            <ArticleTitle>Defining the ascending ramus: A search.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-3913(17)30644-3</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.prosdent.2017.09.016</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STATEMENT OF PROBLEM" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Lack of an accepted definition for the ascending ramus of the mandible means no common reference point is available for clinical or research dialogue.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this review was to determine whether the ascending ramus has been defined, by using a search of published studies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">PubMed was searched, using terms &quot;ascending ramus&quot; and &quot;mandible.&quot;</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The search found no acceptable definition of the ascending ramus of the mandible.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">An acceptable definition for the ascending ramus of the mandible is lacking, and one is proposed here.</AbstractText>
